liticians need all the space  in hell  so  i said we'll we'll take you you die you  go to heaven you're at the pearly gates  saint peter's negotiating with you but  and then saying peter says oh yeah  aren't you the guy that said the pope is  jewish  and then you have this great debate  with  saint peter about that line and we can  really build that  we kicked it around for a little while  didn't use it on that line we used it  later on a routine called  showbiz heaven  but  the line still hung there  and what really was the catalyst for it  was that i had a bunch of dog-eared  paperback thrift store books  of the authors of that day anne rand  phillip wiley john steinbeck pearl  bought vance packet  lenny  didn't read much in those days later he  read thought books voraciously to defend  himself  but back then he read variety and  hollywood reporter and that was about it  he'd  thumb through these books at my place  and he made me give him a critique about  the book or the author and he'd really  irritate me by saying  taking snap judgments that anne ran was  a dyke  philip wiley was a [ __ ]  john steinbeck was a hawk  and pearl buck was sexually repressed  but he couldn't get a handle on vance  packet because it was non-fiction and he  kept making me go further into books  like the power elite and the hidden  persuaders and  when i had used the term  madison avenue boardroom about 10 times  finally he had an epiphany and he put  his fingers up to make a director's  frame  and said that's it  all religious leaders are on madison  avenue in the boardroom  planning next year's religious hustle  and uh  oral roberts is presiding and there's  billy billy graham and bishop sheen and  norman vincent peale and rabbi weiss and  and all the religious leaders of the day  it was a natural because those religious  leaders of that day all had television  shows  all had  top ratings  and they also  held a tremendous amount of power  and  they used that power in a very  conservative fashion endorsing  conservative politicians  so  the thing kind of  wrote itself and of course  the pope is jewish that line  ended up as the capper because at the  end of this board meeting where a lot of  stuff that i don't have time to go  through it  occurred he gets a phone call from the  pope  yes a yes operator i'll accept the  charges  and  and  no uh  yeah billy graham wants i know if you  can get him one of those ferraro cars  and uh and on and on with the pope  and then the capital the whole routine  is the very last line  where he  looks around suspiciously and says don't  worry they don't know you're jewish  that routine  was  just sparked to a lot of other routines  that we did  the the second coming of  um  a lot of other routines that were based  on  religion are based on the power elite  that  uh religious and political leaders who  were hypocrites and and lenny was the  little boy who said that  the emperor wore no clothes so the end  result was that  all those routines  were actually created  we wrote for three months  and those material in those three months  became the bulk of the material in the  first three fantasy albums and the stuff  that we wrote and it didn't work and we  put it on the shelf  we dug it out years later and it became  material from later years  now  i don't mean to challenge the idea that  lenny  wrote his own material  frankie wrote gag lines for lenny  frankie taught lenny  the harder to impersonate voices  we are all made of the talents of those  people we've been exposed to and who  have helped us along the way  the material for those three albums  with some of the best writing i ever did  subsequently i wrote 12 books i've got  frankie's biography coming out soon  it'll be the 13th one  but  uh  we are all part of  of other people's uh training  and lenny was  unique in that he gave voice  to so many things that the public at  large  wanted to say and didn't have a platform  to say  and he said it in their language  in their time  with the complete honesty of their  their  disenchantment with their leadership  so  it was my privilege if you want more  details about some of the other routines  they're in my book  lenny bros the making of a prophet  and um  i'll be happy to answer any questions  after this  thank you so much  our next speaker is susan silver  a comedy writer for the mayor mary tyler  moore show for maude for bob newhart and  others an npr commentator it's a  pleasure to welcome susan here to the  platform  [Applause]  it's really an honor to be here um  i recently lost a very dear friend of  more than 40 years who happened to be  marty garbas's best friend his name was  gary marshall he was my mentor and my  manager and he was a comedy icon too and  he used to say he wanted to be the  recess in the school day of comedy he  wanted to be the light fun no heavy  message not serious academic kind of  thing and i look around at all of you  serious brilliant academicians and so  i'm the recess of this conference  because the reason i'm here is lenny  bruce's mother fixed me up with him when  i was in college  and i want to go like this and say i'm  blonde but  um  one other thing gary said  in show business we have a term that's  called hyphenates you're a director  slash producer a writer slash director  an actor slash waiter usually  anyway um when i retired from show  business i got very involved with  holocaust related things i worked for  the anti-defamation league i ran their  speaker's bureau and i was the un  observer for the simon wiesenthal center  so gary said i was the original  hyphenate sitcom writer slash nazi  hunter  so i guess i do fit in here because i  see we have panels on the holocaust and  humor  um  i've just finished my memoir about being  a woman in a man's world about being a  woman  writing in  the sort of beginning of feminism where  comedy changed because hopefully of mary  tyler moore  but another thing in my book is i'm like  zelig  and i i should say mizzy like i have  encountered in my life every icon of my  generation it's kind of scary i was in  show business i was in politics and i'm  kind of always in the right place at the  right time and as you can see i'm not  very shy so it all worked out very well  um we have to remember as as christie  said the times we were living in this  was the early 60s i'm much older than i  look  um  and it was a time where don't forget on  television married couples were not even  allowed to be shown in the same bed they  had to sleep in separate beds we didn't  even really know what feminism was until  1970 or 1962 when when betty friedan  wrote her book  and i grew up in a double bind of that i  grew up in milwaukee wisconsin which is  about as  midwest a person you could be and when i  grew up all my friends wanted to go to  college to get their mrs  or they'd be a teacher i wanted to go to  hollywood and be a writer but being an  only child in a jewish protective home i  wasn't allowed to cross the main street  until i was 12.  so i could only get to northwestern my  first two years  and then i escaped to hollywood to  california sim city  i had an uncle out there who was also a  famous writer director producer and man  about town his name was psy howard and i  was living with him if my parents only  knew what it was like there they never  would have sent me but that's another  story which we'll have a reference to  lenny in a minute  um  in doing research on lenny since i was  going to be speaking from my personal  experience i found a really great  article he did for playboy in 1963 about  what would happen if you had to play in  milwaukee  and of course i wouldn't have been  allowed to see them but i just want to  read a few pages of this articles it's  it's so great and it also shows how  strange it was for this little  jewish virgin to meet lenny bruce  let's say i'm working at the crescendo  and he says you know i'll finish a show  some guy will come up to me and say i'm  i'm a club owner i'd like you to work  for me it's a beautiful club you ever  work in milwaukee  lots of people you know there i mean  you'll do great you'll kill them you'll  have a lot of fun do you bowl  the only thing is i know that those  clubs between los angeles and new york  the people in the audience are a little  older than me the most i can say to  people over 50 is thank you i've had  enough to eat  so i get to milwaukee the first thing  that frightens me to death is they've  got a 6 30 dinner show  and this was the home of my parents were  always talking about the early bird  special  6 30 lenny says in the afternoon people  are going to a nightclub it's not even  dark yet  i don't want to go in the house it's not  dark yet man if the dinner show is held  up it's only because the jello is not  hard  the people look familiar but i've never  been to milwaukee before  then i realized these are the gray lines  sightseeing bus tours before they leave  it's where they come from  they're like 40 year old chicks with  prom gowns on  they don't laugh they don't heckle they  just stare at me in disbelief and there  are walkouts every night the owner says  to me well i never saw you do that  religious bit and those words you use  the chef is confused the desserts aren't  moving  i go to the men's room i see kids in  there kids four years old kids the kids  are in awe of the men's room this is the  first time they've been in a place their  mother isn't allowed to go in  come on out no uh-uh i'm going to come  in and get you no you're not allowed in  here because everybody's doing making  wet in here  in between the shows i'm a walker i'm  getting nervy nervous and nudgy the  owner decides to cushion me in his next  introduction ladies and gentlemen the  star of our show lenny bruce he's  incidentally a gi an xgi a hell of a  good performer folks a great kidder you  know it's all a bunch of silliness up  here he doesn't mean what he says  he kids about the ho the pope about  jewish religion about colored people  white people it's silly it's a  make-believe he's a hell of a nice guy  he was at the veterans hospital today  for a show he did a show for the boys  here he is his mother's here he's  probably  he's probably from here she lives here  in town really  now a joke is a joke right folks what  the hell i wish that you'd try to  cooperate whoever has been sticking ice  picks in the tires outside it's not  funny  now lenny may kid about narcotics  homosexuality things like that and he  gets walkouts  so that's the world i came from  and there i was this little scared  jewish virgin  the night i met lenny bruce  so this is there's only one person who  has their own chapter in my book of all  the famous people that's lenny bruce  this is my chapter on lenny  it's new year's eve 1964 1965 i can't  remember  i was out with a group of friends at  someone's house and a lively older woman  was entertaining us  she was sally marr a former stripper but  best known as the mother of lenny bruce  lenny the brilliant but troubled  comedian single-handedly changed comedy  with his stream of consciousness his  cultural critique he didn't tell jokes  he told truths dramatic looking with  dark soulful eyes and a sad smile he was  someone i admired  and yes had a crush on  imagine my joy and alarm  when lenny's mother took a liking to me  and said you should meet my son i'm  going to call him and tell him to come  to the party  what  lenny bruce controversial potentially  dangerous  possibly druggy coming to meet a jewish  virgin  okay as long as no one tells my mother  his mother did call him and he came i  wondered why he didn't have a better  place to be on new year's eve  he walked in and it was smitten at first  sight on my side at least i sat at his  feet with a bunch of other girls as he  mulled different topics he pointed to  the angels peeking out from the filigree  borders of the ceiling according to  lenny they now look shocked as they  listen to him  we thought this was a brilliant  observation have not having not even  noticed them ourselves  everything he said was brilliant and by  the end of the evening i was in love or  some crush-like facsimile thereof  he asked for my number and said maybe i  could come down to his show the  following week at the club he played in  hollywood called the crescendo  i said yes of course  then i called my uncle by that time i  was living in an apartment but i  remained close to him and he said no way  this guy is way too dangerous for you i  asked why  he listed about ten reasons  and then he said if i insisted on going  out with lenny bruce he was coming along  as chaperone  and he did  he went with me to the show which was  racy a little scary and amazing my uncle  really admired lenny i was kind of glad  that he had accompanied me because now i  was really scared to death the secret  garden beckoned  we waited at the bar when lenny emerged  we all had a drink  he and my uncle got along famously i sat  there not saying a word out of my  element and when he went and he wrote  something on a napkin and he handed it  to me with a smile  it was a little drawing an arrow with  writing along  before it went around and it said  susan will go to the corner but she's  afraid to go around  wow was it that evident  after we talked to wow who went  backstage and brought out a few pages of  one of his routines and gave them to me  he signed it clark gable found a live in  argentina love lenny bruce  it turned out to be the famous bit about  how to make your colored friends  comfortable at parties you're supposed  to drop names like uncle ben oh i love  that rice aunt jemima oh the maple syrup  you dropped that into the conversation  it was classic and became a collector's  item  which i never would have sold of course  years later it was stolen from my house  in california  i was shocked when not too long after  our date lenny bruce died he odd in a  gruesome way alone in his bathroom  he had been dating another jewish  college girl named lotus weinstock who  was a comic  my uncle couldn't stop from saying i  told you so  frankly thank god he had or who knows  what kind of trouble i'd have gotten  into  but truth be told  i wanted to take care of him he had the  most soulful eyes he was so gentle  and he was a gentleman  he knew that he scared me to death and  he gave me a graceful way out  now a few years later i worked with mort  saul who we've talked about mort was not  a sweet soul  he still isn't um he's still on he's on  twitter now doing his routine in a chair  it's bizarre anyway i i worked for six  months i was the i started i was  assistant by the end of the six months  everybody ahead of me had been fired and  i was the producer of his talk show  because you weren't allowed to talk when  mort talked or you weren't allowed to do  anything that annoyed him we we became  friends later but anyway mort told the  story how he and lenny used to work  together upstairs and downstairs of a  club and half the time lenny was  arrested so mort had to go and do his  show both places  then later in my career i worked with  marvin worth who was kind of the  take care of of the estate of lenny and  he produced the movie  in which dustin hoffman did a good job  but i always wanted to tell marvin who  thought lenny was the sweetest kindest  person he ever knew dustin hoffman was  not nearly as sexy as lenny was  [Applause]  this weekend on american history tv on  c-span 3  this afternoon just before 5 eastern  architecture
 

